2. General
----------
uZDL is written in C++ and uses the Qt 6 framework. To be able to compile uZDL
you need:

    1) Qt 6.5 or newer, including the Widgets module

    2) CMake 3.21 or newer

    3) A C++17 compiler

uZDL also uses SimpleWFA and miniz libraries which are statically linked and
included with the sources. There is nothing else to fetch.

After compiling, see README.md for instructions on using uZDL.

3. Compile
----------
The build is the same on every platform:

	cmake -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<path to your Qt 6 installation>
	cmake --build build --config Release

The resulting binary is 'uzdl' ('uzdl.exe' on Windows).

CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH can be omitted if Qt 6 is installed somewhere CMake already
looks, which is usually the case for Qt installed through a Linux package
manager. For an installation made by the Qt online installer the path looks
like C:/Qt/6.11.2/msvc2022_64 on Windows or ~/Qt/6.11.2/gcc_64 on Linux.

Qt also ships a 'qt-cmake' wrapper that fills CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH in for you:

	<path to Qt>/bin/qt-cmake -B build
	cmake --build build --config Release

4. Platform notes
-----------------
On Windows the build additionally compiles SimpleWFA and the file association
dialog, and embeds the icons and version resource from res/Win32. Visual
Studio 2019 or newer is required, and it must match the Qt 6 toolchain you
installed: Qt 6 binaries built for MSVC cannot be linked with MinGW, nor the
other way around.

On other platforms file associations are unavailable and are compiled out via
the _ZDL_NO_WFA define, which CMake sets for you.

Whether the result is a standalone executable is a property of the Qt build
you link against rather than of this project. Linking a statically built Qt
produces a single-file executable with no extra flags here; the stock dynamic
Qt does not, and its libraries have to be deployed alongside the binary.

5. Packaging a release
----------------------
On Windows the build tree has a 'dist' target:

	cmake --build build --config Release --target dist

It writes build/dist/uZDL-<version>-win64.zip: the executable, the Qt and
MSVC runtime files it needs (collected with windeployqt), README.md,
CHANGELOG, LICENSE and AUTHORS. It deliberately holds no uzdl_portable.ini:
unpacking a newer version over an old folder must never replace the
configuration, so portable mode remains something the user opts into by
creating that file. The version comes from the project() line in
CMakeLists.txt; keep it in step with ZDL_VERSION_STRING in
include/zdlcommon.h, the four version fields in res/Win32/qzdl.rc (a UTF-16
file, so grep will not find them) and the date line at the top of CHANGELOG.

The GitHub Actions workflow in .github/workflows/release.yml runs the same
target on a Windows runner. Pushing a tag named v<version> builds the
package and publishes it as a GitHub release, with the top CHANGELOG entry
as the release notes; running the workflow by hand just keeps the zip as a
workflow artifact. So a release is: bump the version in the four places
above, commit, then

	git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "uZDL 1.0.0"
	git push origin master v1.0.0

